Consider the following version of **Bubble Sort**:

```
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (int j = 0; j < n - 1; j++) {
        if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
            swap(a[j], a[j + 1]);
        }
    }
}
```

Given an array `a`, determine the **total number of swaps** that occur while sorting it with this algorithm.

Given a reference of a node in a **connected** undirected graph, return a **deep copy** (clone) of the graph.

Each node in the graph contains a value (`int`) and a list (`List[Node]`) of its neighbors:
```
class Node {
    public int val;
    public List<Node> neighbors;
}
```
The graph is given via the reference to the first node. Test cases use an adjacency-list representation; `node.val` equals the node's 1-indexed position.
